WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:09.520
N0thing is one of NA’s biggest CS:GO players.

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:17.660
(Casting)

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:21.220
But what does it mean to be the best in NA?

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:28.240
To be perennially at the top of a region that can’t compete with the scene’s top international talent?

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:36.680
(Casting)

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:42.240
What becomes of your legacy when winning is almost always just out of reach?

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:50.320
(Casting)

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:56.540
What becomes of your legacy, when the team you helped build finally wins without you?

00:00:56.540 --> 00:01:02.840
Across a decade of playing CS professionally, N0thing saw it all.

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:07.280
The highs, the lows, and every crushing loss in between.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:11.600
And yet, his legacy has remained untarnished.

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:14.060
This is his story.

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:31.500
Jordan “n0thing” Gilbert was born on October 25, 1990 in San Diego, California, and was

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:33.860
the youngest of three brothers.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:39.160
Like many of today’s esports pros, n0thing got his start in his game of choice by watching

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:41.799
his brother, Jared, play Counter-Strike.

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:46.780
But even before that, n0thing always had a flare for competition, and wasn’t going

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.280
to let other kids walk all over him.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:53.360
"We joined this little roller hockey league, kids were kinda picking on him a little bit

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:58.880
so I went over to kind of protect him a little bit and he pushed me back and he goes "I got this.""

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:05.500
But at home, n0thing was obsessed with video games, specifically, Counter-Strike 1.6.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:08.360
"I home schooled Jordan for the 12 years he was in school,

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:10.880
on his off time he wanted to play video games.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:13.560
How could I say no when he was doing all of his work.

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:19.140
When I saw how effortlessly he was improving and growing so fast,

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:23.320
I just decided that I was going to go as far as he wanted to go with it."

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:30.379
He was playing 10-12 hours of CS a day, nonstop, and then going out to small LAN tournaments

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:31.860
and rolling people.

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:36.520
The only problem was that n0thing was much younger than his adult opponents.

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:41.540
"A lot of the older kids that were playing did not like the fact that they were getting waxed by

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:48.520
a nine or ten year old player so his older brother stood by to make sure things didn't get out of hand, which they

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:49.520
did a few times."

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:54.140
N0thing knew that the only way to make it big was to play as much as possible in front

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:59.500
of as many people as possible, so he leaned on his mother, who drove him to LAN after

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:03.040
LAN, all on n0thing’s quest to become the best.

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:07.560
"So one of the biggest things for me, succeeding in the local scene and probably getting the opportunities

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:08.400
I did.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:12.080
"Was getting notoriety from competing in tournaments in LA and Las Vegas.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:15.600
And my mom was a key factor in getting me there."

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:21.269
And it t all paid off a few years later, when N0thing signed with Evil Geniuses at just

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:22.720
18 years old.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:28.720
"So at that point Amazing Gaming won the KODE5 qualifier and got picked up by Evil Geniuses.

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:33.680
And that was my first pro contract that actually had a salary and they gave me gear and stuff."

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:38.900
For the next four years, EG were the best North American 1.6 team.

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:46.440
They won the ESEA Season 3 Finals, the IEM IV American Finals, the ESEA Season 5 Finals,

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:49.480
ESEA Season 6 Finals and the Beat It!

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:50.980
2010 Finals.

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:56.140
But when it came to international competition, EG always faltered.

00:03:56.580 --> 00:04:09.600
(Casting)

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:12.900
All the while, n0thing was developing a reputation.

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:18.650
He had an aggressive, high-octane style that made him thrilling to watch, and his young

00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:22.080
age made his Wunderkind status all the more impressive.

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:27.680
"I'm gonna be showing you guys today a bit about M4 gun control and just showing you the different aspects

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:32.600
of it and basically all the different battling techniques, you'll need for the different distances,

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:34.680
and situations you'll encounter."

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:40.080
But after so many years atop NA, EG finally crumbled in 2011.

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:45.280
The team fell to 9th-10th at IEM Hannover, then failed to make it out of groups at IEM

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:52.310
VI Global Challenge New York, and capped off the year by placing third at the WCG Pan-American

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:53.040
tournament.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:57.040
During that same time, N0thing was pretty uncertain about his life.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:01.040
EG was paying him a salary, but it wasn't enough to live off.

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:05.440
So he started going to college and even got a marketing internship at Gunnar.

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:08.080
But the internship, just made him jealous.

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:12.720
"It was kind of a weird blow to me ego because I was like dealing into the gaming industry

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:17.680
but in like this behind the scenes way, I was like helping other pro gamers from League and StarCraft

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:21.600
do cool stuff. I remember being jealous, like "Man I wish my game had more traction."

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:27.669
Evil Geniuses left Counter-Strike in January 2012, disbanding their team after months of

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:29.520
rumors and speculation.

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:32.069
That left n0thing in the lurch.

00:05:32.069 --> 00:05:38.720
1.6 was on the way out, and CS:GO, the game’s
sequel, was quickly becoming the game of choice.

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:43.800
But GO wasn’t perfect and, when it came out, n0thing wasn’t even sure if he liked it.

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:49.160
"I hate how in CS:GO, they made it more about "strategy"."

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:54.120
"And less about f*cking just easy, like in 1.6 the recoil was obviously easier to control,

00:05:54.500 --> 00:05:58.780
But the strategy was much more prevalent cause you could actually hold angles, you can't just f*cking

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:03.760
run up like an idiot by yourself. This game, f*cking pure idiot bait, just works everywhere."

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:06.100
But  what n0thing didn’t realize at that time,

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:09.020
was that CS:GO was perfect for him.

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:14.300
"Very early on there was a clip of him like, actually kind of criticising certain aspects of the game

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:18.880
And while he's talking and having a conversation, he's just casually getting four kills in a round.

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:23.500
Just running forward you know, it just seemed so effortless to him. So we all thought that Jordan would

00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:26.260
just be incredible at this game because it just seemed to fit his style so much."

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:30.920
N0thing bounced around  several rosters as CS:GO started to gain traction,

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:37.240
playing with some of NA’s best rising talent. His last team, Team Dynamic, was quickly noticed

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:41.160
and signed by long-running esports org CompLexity Gaming.

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:47.500
The team played in the first two CS:GO Majors, brand new tournaments with massive prize pools

00:06:47.509 --> 00:06:51.950
funded by Valve, making into the Top 8 both times.

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:54.760
And n0thing was popping off.

00:06:54.840 --> 00:07:04.240
(Casting)

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:10.120
After just a few of months of playing GO, that complexity roster changed hands again.

00:07:10.120 --> 00:07:15.640
This time they were acquired by up-and-coming
esports org Cloud9, the team that n0thing

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:18.640
would spend the rest of his professional career with.

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:25.080
CS:GO was growing in ways that 1.6 could never match.

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:30.000
Esports was growing at a rapid pace thanks to growing prize pool, more interest from

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:33.600
sponsors and increased viewership on Twitch.

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:40.000
That meant there was a legion of brand new CS fans, kids who never played CS 1.6, and

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:42.229
were looking for a team to love.

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:46.160
And Cloud9 gave NA fans that team.

00:07:46.200 --> 00:08:01.760
(Casting)

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:07.240
The team was among the best in NA in those early days of CS:GO, but perhaps more importantly,

00:08:07.240 --> 00:08:10.880
n0thing was providing fans with a look behind the curtain.

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:12.480
"What do you gotta say right now."

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:16.240
"All-star game's going live in about 30 minutes."

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:17.840
"What do you think we're gonna win by?"

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:19.920
"16-1?"

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:24.800
"Robin are you playing? Are you playing all-star game?"

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:31.360
N0thing would constantly vlog and take behind-the-scenes videos early on in CS:GO, endearing himself

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:36.280
to fans, and bringing them closer to the team and his goofy personality.

00:08:36.280 --> 00:08:38.120
"Bout to get on another plane,

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:41.360
Going to Stockholm to play a lot of CS and

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:42.560
get insane."

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:52.280
Unfortunately, that was a lot of what there was to like about C9.

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:58.040
They were one of NA’s best, but just like EG, they crumbled in the face of international

00:08:58.080 --> 00:08:59.080
competition.

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:11.560
(Casting)

00:09:11.560 --> 00:09:13.960
Cloud9 placed 9th-12th at

00:09:13.960 --> 00:09:20.480
DreamHack Winter and ESL One Katowice 2015, and until the Spring, they continued to be

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:24.630
stymied by European teams at every tournament they attended.

00:09:24.630 --> 00:09:32.080
In April, C9 made a switch, bringing on Skadoodle and Freakazoid to replace Shahzam and Semphis.

00:09:32.080 --> 00:09:39.600
Then, after a few weeks of feeling out the roster, they were suddenly on the upswing.

00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:56.080
(Casting)

00:09:56.080 --> 00:10:01.900
And they weren’t just the best team in NA, they were finally hanging with the European teams.

00:10:01.900 --> 00:10:14.020
(Casting)

00:10:14.020 --> 00:10:16.660
Unfortunately, they weren’t winning.

00:10:16.660 --> 00:10:29.080
(Casting)

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:34.980
Cloud9 were the undisputed best team in NA and while they could compete internationally,

00:10:34.980 --> 00:10:39.680
they weren’t ever able to push themselves the rest of the way past the finish line.

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:50.800
(Casting)

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:55.760
That summer, they were perennially second-place finishers, behind the European teams that

00:10:55.760 --> 00:10:57.880
beat them, time and time again.

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:08.460
The summer of C9 came to an end at the two Majors that fall, as C9 placed 9th-12th back-to-back

00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:11.280
at both ESL One Cologne 2015

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:20.720
(Casting)

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:23.600
DreamHack Cluj-Napoca 2015.

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:39.760
(Casting)

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:46.300
Shortly thereafter, team captain SeanGares left the team, and was replaced for the 2016 season.

00:11:46.520 --> 00:11:52.100
And, at least for the first few months, the roster move didn’t go according to plan.

00:11:52.300 --> 00:12:01.420
(Casting)

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:09.440
At MLG Columbus, Cloud9 watched as Team Liquid rose up and placed 3rd-4th, the highest finish

00:12:09.440 --> 00:12:12.950
an NA team had ever attained at a Major.

00:12:12.950 --> 00:12:16.760
Meanwhile, Cloud9 were out in dead last.

00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:23.200
C9’s place as NA’s best team was officially taken over, and the team went into a roster

00:12:23.200 --> 00:12:24.200
freefall.

00:12:24.200 --> 00:12:30.170
They lost Freakazoid, added adreN, then Slemmy,
then Autimatic, all while getting rid of their

00:12:30.170 --> 00:12:32.040
manager and coach.

00:12:32.040 --> 00:12:36.300
The Cloud9 that ruled NA was well and truly done.

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:41.840
In its place, there was a new Cloud9, one that, for a while, looked pretty solid.

00:12:42.060 --> 00:12:54.500
(Casting)

00:12:54.500 --> 00:12:59.120
They were a team that could once again, compete with the top international teams.

00:12:59.240 --> 00:13:08.820
(Casting)

00:13:08.820 --> 00:13:10.660
But they couldn’t win.

00:13:10.720 --> 00:13:34.800
(Casting)

00:13:34.900 --> 00:13:39.020
At least, not until the ESL Pro League Season 4 Finals.

00:13:39.460 --> 00:13:59.240
(Casting)

00:13:59.440 --> 00:14:05.180
Cloud9 went on a tear at the EPL Season 4 Finals, and in a complete upset,

00:14:05.320 --> 00:14:11.500
brought down home-town favourites SK Gaming in front of a crowd of cheering Brazilian fans.

00:14:11.740 --> 00:14:40.100
(Casting)

00:14:40.200 --> 00:14:48.420
"I'm trying not to cry, I've played this game for a long time. To beat these guys here means a lot to me,

00:14:48.420 --> 00:14:51.880
and my team played amazing you know.

00:14:51.880 --> 00:14:54.840
I can say all the cliche things right now I'm just really f*cking excited."

00:14:56.160 --> 00:14:58.480
Things were looking up for Cloud9.

00:14:58.480 --> 00:15:03.600
They made it into the Atlanta Major closed qualifier, and were back to playing well in NA,

00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:06.580
despite their continued failures internationally.

00:15:06.980 --> 00:15:10.880
But n0thing helped C9 claw their way back up the ladder.

00:15:11.140 --> 00:15:17.040
They beat SK again at the Subaru Invitational, qualified for the Major with a first place

00:15:17.050 --> 00:15:23.310
finish at the minor and 3rd-5th at the closed qualifier, and even took a shocking second-place

00:15:23.320 --> 00:15:26.600
finish at ESL One Cologne 2017.

00:15:27.160 --> 00:15:39.080
(Casting)

00:15:39.080 --> 00:15:42.880
But once again, it all came crashing down at the Major.

00:15:42.880 --> 00:16:03.040
(Casting)

00:16:03.040 --> 00:16:06.600
Cloud9 left the Krakow Major in 9th-11th place.

00:16:06.600 --> 00:16:11.920
Not dead last this time, but not good enough to guarantee a spot in the next Major.

00:16:11.920 --> 00:16:16.960
They needed a change, and all eyes were on its oldest members.

00:16:20.080 --> 00:16:27.760
On August 15, 2017, just a month after their catastrophic Major exit, Cloud9 dropped Shroud

00:16:27.760 --> 00:16:33.040
and n0thing, the last remaining founding member of C9’s CS:GO team.

00:16:33.040 --> 00:16:37.200
"And so I had a feeling it was one or the other, it was either we were going to make a change with someone

00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:41.380
else, or we're gonna make a change with you. So he said it, I said "Alright baby, that's all I need to know."

00:16:41.380 --> 00:16:45.260
It almost was a relief because we were almost in this limbo for a while as a team.

00:16:45.260 --> 00:16:46.660
We needed something to change.

00:16:46.840 --> 00:16:52.340
Shroud, for his part, had been talking about retirement for over a year, and was ready

00:16:52.340 --> 00:16:54.480
to live the streaming lifestyle.

00:16:54.880 --> 00:16:58.480
"In my eyes, I'm actually set for 2018."

00:17:00.880 --> 00:17:02.240
"Are you trolling?"

00:17:03.120 --> 00:17:10.080
"No I'm serious, I've told pretty much everyone this. Like one more solid year."

00:17:10.160 --> 00:17:12.600
But n0thing approached things differently.

00:17:12.600 --> 00:17:17.360
He had been playing CS professionally for over a decade when he got benched, for perhaps

00:17:17.360 --> 00:17:20.120
the first time in his entire life.

00:17:20.120 --> 00:17:26.860
He had gone from wunderkind superstar, to
early CS:GO adopter, to beloved personality

00:17:26.880 --> 00:17:30.240
and then all of a sudden, people thought he
was washed up.

00:17:30.400 --> 00:17:36.880
"Kind of sad to see n0thing go out, but I guess, like even if you're a critic of Tarik

00:17:37.120 --> 00:17:44.000
Tarik for n0thing obviously still feels like an upgrade for me, and Rush I think is f*cking mental.

00:17:44.000 --> 00:17:45.360
I think he's insanely good."

00:17:45.360 --> 00:17:49.600
Meanwhile, his contract with Cloud9 meant that he wasn’t able to play for another

00:17:49.680 --> 00:17:53.840
team until May 2018, nearly a year away.

00:17:53.840 --> 00:17:58.870
So he decided to double down on bringing his personality to the fans.

00:17:58.880 --> 00:18:04.240
"About to go zipline, we're about to go eat lunch actually. She's pretty scared. I've done it before done in

00:18:04.240 --> 00:18:05.600
South America?"

00:18:05.600 --> 00:18:09.080
"Well it's going to be fun, so hopefully we get some good shots on the helmet.

00:18:09.080 --> 00:18:12.920
If not I'll be really sad, maybe I'll sneak the gopro in my pocket if I can't fit it on the mount.

00:18:12.920 --> 00:18:14.600
And then just whip it out and record."

00:18:14.760 --> 00:18:16.520
That meant more streaming

00:18:16.720 --> 00:18:28.480
(Stream)

00:18:28.480 --> 00:18:31.880
And even getting in front of the camera as an analyst.

00:18:31.880 --> 00:18:35.080
"Dude VP's always had for quite a while a playbook.

00:18:35.080 --> 00:18:37.760
When they were in their prime it was pretty wild to play them on maps like Cobble cause they

00:18:37.760 --> 00:18:39.800
almost had a different playbook depending on who they played."

00:18:39.880 --> 00:18:47.880
But while he was re-defining his identity in the CS scene, Cloud9 was redefining their own identity in CS.

00:18:47.880 --> 00:18:52.600
"I don't expect us to win every tournament, I don't expect us to make it out of groups every time.

00:18:52.600 --> 00:18:58.360
I do expect us to compete at a really high level though, making it hard for the teams to beat us.

00:18:58.360 --> 00:19:02.960
Making us unpredictable, maybe grabbing a few wins here and there, hopefully we can win the Major.

00:19:02.960 --> 00:19:08.440
After falling apart in Krakow, the new roster was sharp, and went on a tear.

00:19:08.480 --> 00:19:11.040
3rd-4th at ESL One New York

00:19:14.720 --> 00:19:15.840
ELEAGUE

00:19:16.080 --> 00:19:26.080
(Casting)

00:19:26.080 --> 00:19:27.680
And IEM Oakland.

00:19:27.680 --> 00:19:33.360
"Alright then the semifinal's about to happen. Let's just hear it from you, who's winning this is it FaZe?

00:19:35.840 --> 00:19:37.360
So is it Cloud9?

00:19:38.480 --> 00:19:42.560
Let's get it on it's semifinal time!"

00:19:42.960 --> 00:19:47.840
Cloud9 was looking like the best in NA again, and while they weren’t winning every significant

00:19:47.900 --> 00:19:53.360
tournament, they were placing well and truly
competing with international rosters.

00:19:53.360 --> 00:20:00.300
And then, at the Boston Major, Cloud9 shocked the world by bringing down FaZe Clan 2-1 in

00:20:00.300 --> 00:20:05.400
the Grand Finals, becoming the first ever NA Major Champions.

00:20:05.600 --> 00:20:25.780
(Casting)

00:20:25.780 --> 00:20:28.200
But they did it without n0thing.

00:20:28.200 --> 00:20:33.340
N0thing isn’t necessarily upset that his team won the major without him.

00:20:33.350 --> 00:20:37.240
In fact, in a certain light, it’s not really his team anyways.

00:20:37.240 --> 00:20:41.380
It’s not the team he built, but the org he stuck with.

00:20:41.380 --> 00:20:46.919
Not being there for the win hurt, sure, but the way the team fell apart afterwards proved

00:20:46.920 --> 00:20:50.880
to him that maybe stepping back from the team was the right call.

00:20:51.140 --> 00:20:55.240
"And even thinking about it, obviously just wish I won a Major like every player wishes they won a Major.

00:20:55.240 --> 00:20:57.140
but not like "Ah f*ck these guys."

00:20:57.140 --> 00:20:59.820
I think it was actually best that the team made those decisions,

00:20:59.820 --> 00:21:02.260
to part ways, I probably should have left the team earlier.

00:21:02.260 --> 00:21:05.720
or changed something on the team earlier when I had a little bit more,

00:21:05.980 --> 00:21:08.620
of a say in what would happen."

00:21:12.640 --> 00:21:15.200
N0thing has gone back to play, just a little.

00:21:15.200 --> 00:21:20.800
Most notably, he stood in for Mousesports at ESL One Belo Horizonte, and helped the

00:21:20.880 --> 00:21:23.440
team to a second place finish in Brazil.

00:21:23.440 --> 00:21:28.400
"Yeah I mean I'm here just trying to play some CS, I'm definitely not replacing Oskar,

00:21:28.400 --> 00:21:31.840
that guy's a god awper, he's got a special role in this team.

00:21:31.840 --> 00:21:36.800
I'm just happy to be here, and not cause them any problems. Be able to stay with the pace they wanna play

00:21:36.800 --> 00:21:42.500
I'm proud to say that without a lot of practice I'm still able to show up and do what's necessary to beat top

00:21:42.500 --> 00:21:45.700
teams in the world, and I'm just really happy to play in front of this great crowd."

00:21:45.740 --> 00:21:50.600
But while playing for Mouz, n0thing got a chance to play with ropz, the team’s rising

00:21:50.600 --> 00:21:57.040
young star who cites n0thing as an influence for why he got into CS in the first place.

00:21:57.040 --> 00:21:59.640
"I mean I started playing 1.6 when I was around 7,

00:21:59.640 --> 00:22:03.520
And that was around the time when n0thing was like the same age as me,

00:22:03.640 --> 00:22:09.320
And he had like a lot of these good matches and frag movies,

00:22:09.320 --> 00:22:11.840
which I really still look at nowadays.

00:22:12.040 --> 00:22:15.800
And yeah he's probably the person I look up to."

00:22:15.880 --> 00:22:17.680
as for what comes next?

00:22:17.680 --> 00:22:20.240
N0thing says that’s still up in  the air.

00:22:20.240 --> 00:22:22.880
"I've created kind of a little ecosystem,

00:22:22.880 --> 00:22:28.800
and that's what I'm doing. I'm not opposed to like playing again but the problem is is a lot of teams

00:22:28.880 --> 00:22:31.280
want a certain level of commitment.

00:22:31.280 --> 00:22:36.000
So all these things are happening and then I'm advising a couple companies passively.

00:22:36.000 --> 00:22:43.440
and I've put out this on my stream a couple times, I thought it'd be awesome if I were to play again

00:22:43.440 --> 00:22:50.380
to create an org, but you know building an org isn't like "Hey you wanna make an org, like alright cool see ya."

00:22:50.380 --> 00:22:55.200
Which, in itself, is a sign of what n0thing has become to CS fans.

00:22:55.280 --> 00:23:00.360
It’s been a long time since n0thing was the young hotshot savant he was known for

00:23:00.360 --> 00:23:02.080
being in 1.6.

00:23:02.080 --> 00:23:05.820
It’s been a long time since he was one of the best players in NA.

00:23:05.820 --> 00:23:10.120
It’s been a long time since he’s even been on a successful roster.

00:23:10.120 --> 00:23:13.779
But there is something about n0thing that is always true.

00:23:13.780 --> 00:23:16.720
N0thing will always be a crowd favourite.

00:23:16.720 --> 00:23:22.299
His legacy is one of excellence, but perhaps more importantly, of a player who wasn’t

00:23:22.299 --> 00:23:28.040
afraid to pull down the curtain a little, be honest with his fans and teach people about the game.

00:23:28.040 --> 00:23:30.760
"So what's cool for me as a streamer right now,

00:23:30.760 --> 00:23:34.200
is to keep educating, keep pushing kids to get better cause,

00:23:34.400 --> 00:23:41.640
really what keeps the scene thriving, is all the large group of players, the not super competitive guys

00:23:41.640 --> 00:23:46.360
having fun playing it because they are trying to get better. Or whatever reason to play with their friends

00:23:46.360 --> 00:23:47.680
and sense of community so."

00:23:47.680 --> 00:23:53.480
N0thing’s legacy in CS:GO will likely remain forever, but the work he does now, in that

00:23:53.480 --> 00:23:59.520
liminal state between player and retired personality, is sealing him in as one of NA’s most important

00:23:59.520 --> 00:24:01.040
CS personalities.

00:24:01.040 --> 00:24:06.600
The educating he does, the fans he keeps interested in the game, and the ability he has to inspire

00:24:06.600 --> 00:24:12.080
young players will only become  more important to the scene as it matures.

00:24:16.360 --> 00:24:21.280
Thanks for watching. If you want more great content just like this, be sure to hit the subscribe button.

